"views](../reference/string-views.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:28
msgid "Variables: `let` and `let mut`"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:30
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"vars\");\n"
"    let a = 10;          // an immutable binding\n"
"    let mut b = 10;      // a mutable binding\n"
"    b = 20;              // OK — b may be reassigned\n"
"    // a = 30;           // ERROR: a is not `mut`\n"
"    log.info(f\"a={a} b={b}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:47
msgid ""
"`mut` is permission to write _through that name_: reassigning the binding, "
"assigning to a field (`p.x = 3`), or assigning to an element (`xs[0] = 7`). "
"Sharing is not gated by `mut` — two bindings can name the same value, and "
"what `mut` controls is only who may write."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:52
msgid ""
"Every type has a zero value: `0` for numbers, `false` for `bool`, the empty "
"string, `nil` for nullables. Omit a field in a struct literal and it takes "
"the field's initializer if there is one, else the type's zero value (see "
"[structs, enums, and classes](types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:57
msgid "Numbers"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:59
msgid ""
"An unsuffixed integer literal defaults to `i32`; an unsuffixed float "
"defaults to `f32`. The default is also a _ceiling_: a literal adapts to the "
"expected type only while it fits the default."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:63
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"numbers\");\n"
"    let a = 10;                        // i32\n"
"    let b = 10u8;                      // u8 via suffix\n"
"    let c: u64 = 10;                   // u64 via annotation — 10 fits\n"
"    let big = 18446744073709551615u64; // past the i32 default: suffix "
"required\n"
"    let d = 1.5;                       // f32\n"
"    let d64: f64 = 1.5;                // f64 via annotation\n"
"    let hex = 0xFF_u32;                // 0x / 0b / 0o bases, _ separators\n"
"    log.info(f\"a={a} d={d} d64={d64} hex={hex} big={big}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:83
msgid ""
"Conversions are explicit casts: `expr as T`. Casts truncate like C — they "
"never trap."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:86
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"casts\");\n"
"    let cast = 300 as u8;   // 44 — keeps the low 8 bits\n"
"    log.info(f\"cast={cast}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:100
msgid "Arithmetic has two sharp edges:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:102
msgid ""
"**Mixed widths do not mix.** Both operands must have the same width — "
"convert one side first. `let mut total = 0.0;` makes an `f32`, and adding an "
"`f64` to it is a type error; annotate `let mut total: f64 = 0.0;` when you "
"mean double precision."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:106
msgid ""
"**Overflow, division by zero, and out-of-bounds indexing trap.** For the "
"explicit non-trapping ladder (`wrapping_add`, `saturating_mul`, "
"`checked_add`), see [the standard library](stdlib.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:110
msgid "Text: plain, raw, and format strings"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:112
msgid ""
"Three literal forms. A plain string is always inert — no interpolation ever "
"happens implicitly."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:115
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"text\");\n"
"    let name = \"rut\";\n"
"    let s = \"hi\\tname\";              // plain: escapes processed\n"
"    let raw = r\"C:\\temp\\log.txt\";    // raw: every byte is literal\n"
"    let t = f\"hi {name}!\";           // format: placeholders evaluated\n"
"    log.info(f\"{s} | {raw} | {t}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:132
msgid ""
"Plain and format strings share the same escapes (`\\t \\n"
" \\r \\\\ \\\"` and `\\u{...}`). In an f-string, `{ expr }` splices any "
"expression — identifiers, calls, arithmetic — except nested string literals "
"(bind one to a variable first). `{{` and `}}` are literal braces:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:137
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"text\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"open{{close}} braces\");   // prints: open{close} braces\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:150
msgid ""
"What an f-string can render: integers (decimal), floats (shortest round-trip "
"decimal: `3.5`, `0.1`), `bool` (`true`/`false`), `str` (contents), and enum "
"members (their name: `Color.Green` renders `Green`). Composite values — "
"structs, classes, arrays, vecs — are a compile error inside an f-string; "
"write a `to_string()`\\-style method and call it, or use a debug dump for "
"development."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:157
msgid "Sequences and buffers"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:159
msgid "The fixed array `[T]` is built from a literal or a repeat:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:161
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"arrays\");\n"
"    let arr = [1, 2, 3];         // [i32] — fixed length\n"
"    let zero: [u8] = [0u8; 34];  // 34 slots of 0\n"
"    log.info(f\"len={arr.len()} first={arr[0]} zero.len={zero.len()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:176
msgid ""
"The growable sequence is `Vec<T>` (from the `pouch` package — `use pouch::{ "
"Vec };`): `Vec.new()`, `Vec.from([..])`, `Vec<i32>.filled(0, 1024)`, `push`, "
"`pop`, `len`. Indexing and `for..of` work the same on both. See [the "
"standard library](stdlib.md) for the full surface."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:182
msgid ""
"`bytes` is the binary primitive. `s.encode()` turns text into octets, "
"`b.decode()` reads it back (lossy UTF-8), `bytes.zeroed(n)` and "
"`bytes.from(a)` build buffers directly:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:186
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"bytes\");\n"
"    let b = \"rut runs\".encode();\n"
"    let ok = b.decode() == \"rut runs\";   // true — content comparison\n"
"    log.info(f\"len={b.len()} roundtrip={ok}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:201
msgid "Sharing: the one rule"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:203
msgid ""
"Primitives and `fn` values **copy** on assignment, argument passing, and "
"return. **Every other value is a shared cell**: `let q = p` is an O(1) "
"handle move, and a write through any alias is visible through all of them."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:208
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32 }\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"sharing\");\n"
"    let mut p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n"
"    let q = p;          // q and p name ONE cell\n"
"    p.x = 4;            // q.x is 4 now\n"
"    log.info(f\"q.x={q.x} same cell: {q == p}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:226
msgid "Equality follows the same split:"
msgstr ""

