msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:232
msgid "cell identity — two separately-built literals are never equal"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:234
msgid ""
"The one copy escape hatch is `bytes.clone()` — a fresh buffer with the same "
"octets. There is no generic copy for any other type; if you need a divergent "
"value, build a new one."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:238
msgid ""
"Absence is `nil` on a nullable `?T` — see [errors and "
"optionality](errors.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:241
#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:79
msgid "Tuples"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:243
msgid "Records `(A, B)` are first-class values with numeric fields:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:245
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn divmod(a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, i32) {\n"
"    return (a / b, a % b);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"tuples\");\n"
"    let (q, r) = divmod(17, 5);   // destructuring\n"
"    let t = (1, true);\n"
"    log.info(f\"q={q} r={r} t.0={t.0}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:264
msgid ""
"The pair is also rut's standard error channel — the next chapters use it "
"constantly."
msgstr ""

