#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:3
msgid ""
"A **parse-only** example: `examples/04-custom-async/custom_async.rut` is a "
"single rut file with no Cargo harness — you cannot run it today, and that is "
"a deliberate disclosure, not an oversight. What it shows is the async "
"vocabulary from the _user_ side: a hand-written future type, an `impl` of "
"the engine's built-in `Future` trait, and a user-written launcher with "
"per-checkpoint stats and cancellation audits. The runnable harness that "
"would drive it end to end is the follow-up work; the file is kept in the "
"repo now because the parser's conformance suite compiles every example, so "
"this vocabulary is checked to stay parseable ([Async and "
"await](../reference/async.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:15
msgid "\"Run\" it"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:17
msgid ""
"There is no harness. The file is exercised by the parser conformance test, "
"which walks every `.rut` file in `examples/`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:21
msgid "# parses every example, incl. this one\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:24
msgid ""
"To _read_ the same machinery running, see [06 — GitHub viewer "
"CLI](06-github-viewer-cli.md): its brain is an `async fn` driven by the "
"standard launcher this file re-spells by hand."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:31
msgid "The audit log — the example's reason to exist"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:33
msgid ""
"`CustomFuture` is a stage machine, but the _point_ of the file is the "
"accounting around it: every launch, resumption, cancellation, and completion "
"lands in one module-owned record the embedder can read after the run:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:38
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"struct AuditLog {\n"
"    launches: u32;      // futures this log saw started\n"
"    yields: u32;        // resumptions driven — one per checkpoint\n"
"    cancels: u32;       // cancellation probes that answered true\n"
"    checkpoints: u32;   // high-water mark of the context ledger\n"
"    finished: u32;      // runs that walked every step they booked\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:48
msgid ""
"The launcher takes a shared `?AuditLog`, so the embedder holds one cell and "
"reads the whole run's story out of it afterwards — `summary` renders the "
"one-line audit."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:52
msgid "The machine — what `async fn` weaves, spelled out"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:54
msgid ""
"When you write `async fn`, the engine weaves a hidden frame whose shape is "
"exactly this: a stage counter, the payload the stages carry, and a done "
"flag. `CustomFuture` spells that shape out in source, so you can see what "
"the keyword hides. The machine itself is plain rut — only the _driving "
"harness_ is missing — so the block below adds a `main` that advances it by "
"hand; that is as far as one can run it today:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:62
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct CustomFuture {\n"
"    steps: u32;      // checkpoints booked before the run finishes\n"
"    stage: str;      // what the current stage does (the audit's label)\n"
"    done: bool;      // the last stage ran\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl CustomFuture {\n"
"    fn new(steps: u32, stage: str) -> Self {\n"
"        return Self { steps: steps, stage: stage, done: false };\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    /// One stage of work — what one resumption advances. `false` when\n"
"    /// no stages are left: the runner's completion signal.\n"
"    fn advance(mut self) -> bool {\n"
"        if (self.done) {\n"
"            return false;\n"
"        }\n"
"        if (self.steps == 0) {\n"
"            self.done = true;\n"
"            return false;\n"
"        }\n"
"        self.steps -= 1;\n"
"        return true;\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"future\");\n"
"    let mut f = CustomFuture.new(3, \"poll\");\n"
"    let mut polls = 0;\n"
"    while (f.advance()) {\n"
"        polls += 1;\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"3 steps took {polls} advancing polls, then advance() "
"answered false (done={f.done})\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:106
msgid "The trait surface: a user impl of a built-in trait"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:108
msgid ""
"`Future` is an engine-named built-in trait — the engine weaves it for every "
"`async fn`'s hidden frame — but built-in traits are engine _named_, not "
"engine _closed_. A hand-written machine registers through the ordinary "
"nominal path, one impl block, and rides the same drive law ([Traits and "
"dispatch](../core-concepts/traits-and-dispatch.md)). `Future` and "
"`RunContext` are `pub builtin` core names — the impl names them, so the file "
"imports them (`use core::{ Future, RunContext }`):"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:116
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Future, RunContext };\n"
"\n"
"impl Future<nil> for CustomFuture {\n"
"    fn yield(self, cx: RunContext) {\n"
"        // the ledger view: the frame's resume state, read as data\n"
"        let at = cx.checkpoint();\n"
"        // the cancellation probe: a cancelled frame stops advancing —\n"
"        // the audit sees it as \"died here, did not advance\" — and runs\n"
"        // its drop path instead of the next stage\n"
"        if (cx.cancelled()) {\n"
"            self.done = true;\n"
"            self.stage = f\"{self.stage}@{at}:cancelled\";\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:134
msgid ""
"The receiver **is** the frame — the machine's fields are its state — and the "
"context is the only handle a resumption needs. The frozen context protocol "
"reads as data: `checkpoint` answers this frame's resume state, `cancelled` "
"answers the task's abort flag. Cancellation is a value the frame inspects, "
"not an exception it catches."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:140
msgid "The user launcher"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:142
msgid ""
"The engine's standard launcher set (`launch_future`, `LaunchedFutureHandle`, "
"`sleep`) is ordinary code any module may re-spell — that is the point of "
"`launch_custom`. It supplies the driving and the audit over the same "
"`Future` surface, so the machine never knows which launcher started it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:158
msgid ""
"The returned `CustomLaunched` receipt is deliberately **not** a future — it "
"cannot be awaited, and it is not re-launchable (that is a type error, never "
"a runtime check). Its one real member is the cancel edge, which flags the "
"task and lets the loop's re-drive run the probe:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:182
msgid ""
"Alongside sits `audit_drive`, the audit twin of the engine loop's drive "
"columns: run a stage, count the resumption, stop on completion."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:185
msgid "Scope, honestly"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:187
msgid "Two boundaries to keep straight when you read this file:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:189
msgid ""
"**User futures are launcher-drivable** — the driving loop finds the "
"`Future::yield` row in the vtable, exactly as it does for woven frames."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:192
msgid ""
"**`await` targets engine-woven futures only** in the current build (async-fn "
"results and `sleep`); awaiting an arbitrary user impl, and joining "
"concurrent futures, land with the next async milestone."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:196
msgid ""
"So today the file is a parse-checked design corpus and a teaching document: "
"the shape of the weave, the shape of a launcher, and the proof that the "
"future trait is a user-extensible surface — not a runnable demo. When you "
"want the runnable version of the same story, read [06 — GitHub viewer "
"CLI](06-github-viewer-cli.md), and for the model behind it, [the async "
"model](../core-concepts/async-model.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:205
msgid ""
"`async fn` compiles to a hidden frame implementing `Future` — this file "
"spells that frame out in user source."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:207
msgid ""
"Built-in traits are engine-named, not engine-closed: `impl Future<nil> for "
"YourType` registers through the ordinary path."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:209
msgid ""
"Cancellation is data the frame reads (`cx.cancelled()`), and every "
"checkpoint is observable (`cx.checkpoint()`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:211
msgid ""
"A launcher is just code: driving + audit over the public `Future` surface, "
"re-spellable by any module."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/04-custom-async.md:213
msgid ""
"The file is parse-only by disclosure; the runnable harness is follow-up work "
"— the machine itself is plain rut (it ran above), but nothing launches or "
"drives it as a future yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:3
msgid ""
"One page app — a todolist with a simulated server — where **every DOM move "
"and every timer goes through ten declared host crossings**, and the page's "
"brain is a rut project of **two packages**. `ui` is the framework: an atom "
"store (the jotai shape, in rut), a widget type, a keyed diff, and a "
"component vocabulary. `biz` is the domain: the todolist machine expressed as "
"store mutations, plus the app shell. The host — web-sys on wasm32 in the "
"browser, a fake-DOM twin on native in the tests — owns the loop; rut owns "
"the state. Pure rut: no event loop, no async keywords on the rut side; every "
"asynchronous fact enters through a door named for its event."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:17
msgid "# the twin + the law (95 tests), from the repo root\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:19
msgid "# the through-the-artifact gate (node tier + browser tier)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:22
msgid ""
"`cargo test` runs the whole story on the **fake-DOM twin** — a HashMap "
"element tree with real DOM semantics and a virtual timer clock — over the "
"same rut sources the browser runs. The e2e script builds nothing silently "
"and skips nothing silently: tier 1 (plain node) drives the real wasm "
"artifact through the loader's own ABI on a fake DOM; tier 2 (when "
"geckodriver + Firefox exist) types and clicks the live page."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:30
msgid "To open the page yourself:"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:40
msgid ""
"What you'll see: type a title — the status line echoes the typing and the "
"counts never move (the draft is not a counts dependency). Press Add — an "
"italic _pending_ line paints immediately, the counts line flips to `1 in "
"flight`, and ~400 ms later the committed row replaces it. Toggle a row — the "
"title goes italic for ~250 ms, then the check fills and the title strikes "
"through. The row animates because the keyed diff **kept the node alive** — "
"nothing is cleared and rebuilt, ever."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:51
msgid "The ABI is the page: `main` plus the doors"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:53
msgid ""
"`rut/biz/app.rut` — the boot turn builds the app container and returns it as "
"an `opaque` (rut has no mutable module state, so the host holds the state "
"and re-passes it every turn — the [00 — Todolist](00-todolist.md) pattern at "
"page scale):"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:58
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"entry fn main() -> opaque {\n"
"    let boot: World = world_boot(\"booted — type a title, press Add\");\n"
"    let root = AppRoot {\n"
"        world: boot,\n"
"        t1: t1_mount(\"app\"),\n"
"    };\n"
"    paint(root);\n"
"    return opaque(root);\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:70
msgid ""
"Every asynchronous fact enters through a door named for its event — "
"`on_click`/`on_input` for DOM events, `on_timer` for the clock. Each door "
"re-passes the container and answers the entry-err pair: `(opaque(c), \"\")` "
"on a clean turn, `(nil, why)` on a soft failure the pump reports _while "
"keeping the page alive_."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:76
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"fn dom_event(mut r: AppRoot, id: str, detail: str) -> (?opaque, str) {\n"
"    let booked = t1_event(r.t1, id, detail);\n"
"    let req = opaque.downcast<Req>(booked);\n"
"    if (req != nil) {\n"
"        tim_after(req.latency, req.tag);\n"
"    }\n"
"    paint(r);\n"
"    return (opaque(r), \"\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:88
msgid ""
"The \"server\" is a request table, not a scheduler: adding never touches the "
"list — the machine books a request and the app books one `tim_after(latency, "
"tag)`; the list changes only when the timer's turn runs the answer mutation "
"(per-kind latency, so deadline-ordered commits are visible across kinds)."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:94
msgid "The ten crossings"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:96
msgid ""
"`web.d.rut` (flat at the example root, registered by hand in both lanes) "
"declares the whole host surface. Elements cross as `opaque` handles; "
"everything else is strings, ints, and bools — no app names, no data "
"shipping, no callbacks ([the host "
"boundary](../core-concepts/host-boundary.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:115
msgid ""
"A wiring bug is loud: a missing id traps (`web::ui_get: no element '#x'`), a "
"kind mismatch names both sides. The host never grew an app-specific crossing "
"— the entire widget system fits over these ten rows, unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:120
msgid "The store: jotai's shape over private traits"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:122
msgid ""
"`rut/ui/store.rut` is the kernel. Handles are keys, not objects — "
"`Source<T>`, `Derived<T>`, `Mutation<A, R>` carry ids and have no logic of "
"their own beyond routing through their store. The machinery traits are "
"**module-private**, so the domain package cannot name them even to import "
"them — which is how \"a derived cell is not writable\" is enforced at the "
"type level ([Traits and dispatch](../core-concepts/traits-and-dispatch.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:130
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"trait Readable<T> {\n"
"    fn atom_id(self) -> u32;\n"
"    fn materialize(self, st: Store) -> nil; // first touch: the seed lands\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"trait Writable<A, R> {\n"
"    fn atom_id(self) -> u32;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:141
msgid ""
"Freshness is **pull-on-read**: a write bumps a generation, and a `get` "
"recomputes a stale derived at most once per write-set. There is no flush "
"step anywhere. Dependencies are **discovered, never declared** — the "
"`ctx.get` calls inside a derive closure ARE the dependency list, re-recorded "
"on every recompute. The domain's counts line is the worked example "
"(`rut/biz/world.rut`):"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:148
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"let counts$ = store.derive(fn (ctx) -> str {\n"
"    let items = ctx.get(items$);\n"
"    let reqs = ctx.get(reqs$);\n"
"    let mut open = 0;\n"
"    for (let t of items) {\n"
"        if (t.done == false) {\n"
"            open += 1;\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"    let done_n = items.len() - open;\n"
"    return f\"{open} open | {done_n} done | {reqs.len()} in flight\";\n"
"});\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:163
msgid ""
"Writes go through mutation fns — `add_m`, `toggle_m`, `remove_m`, `answer_m` "
"— multi-step write programs that run in the one write lane; the machine's "
"counters are sources too, because with no domain container there is nowhere "
"else for state to live."
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:168
msgid "Widgets are data; the diff does the DOM"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:170
msgid ""
"`view(r)` is a pure function from app state to a `Widget` tree — it runs no "
"crossing and reads nothing (reads are reactive _props_, resolved at the "
"render door). `t1_render` diffs the new tree against the previous one held "
"in `T1Root` and fires only deltas: match children by key, patch in place, "
"re-append on reorder, retire gone keys' listeners. Nothing changed = nothing "
"fires ([HashMaps and sets](../reference/builtin-generic-types.md) carry the "
"path-keyed tables):"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:179
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"pub struct T1Root {\n"
"    parent: opaque; // the mounted root element\n"
"    prev: ?Widget = nil; // the previous tree — the diff's left side\n"
"    els: HashMap<str, opaque>; // path -> live handle (ops application)\n"
"    regs: HashMap<str, Ev>; // listener id (as crossed) -> the\n"
"    // widget's event wiring (a data-shaped binding)\n"
"    lids: HashMap<str, i64>; // path -> listener id (retirement)\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/examples/05-todolist-web.md:190
