#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:144
msgid "Containers under sharing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:146
msgid ""
"`Vec<T>` backs on `buf: [?T]` — `[nil; cap]` is the generic zero (`nil` is "
"the slot's zero); `push` takes the `T → ?T`\\-boxed handle; loads yield the "
"`?T` (uses auto-deref). See [Builtin generic "
"types](builtin-generic-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:150
msgid ""
"Keyed collections: `get(k) -> ?V` — `nil` = absent, a hit answers the "
"**stored cell** (the aliasing law: two gets of one key name one cell until a "
"replace re-stores)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:153
msgid ""
"Array windows (`v.slice(a, b) -> ?Vec<T>`) are the shared cell: writes "
"through the window hit the parent (see [String slicing and "
"views](string-views.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:156
msgid ""
"Erasure rides the same laws: `opaque(v)` aliases the payload's cell, and "
"`opaque.downcast<T>(o)`'s match is the box's own inner cell (see [opaque — "
"erasure and downcast](opaque.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:160
msgid "The answer channel"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:162
msgid ""
"**`(?T, err)` — concretely `(value, ok)` / `(T, str)` — is THE answer "
"channel.** The convention is law: empty err + a value = success; empty err + "
"`nil` = \"not found\"; non-empty err = \"failed\". The err channel "
"disambiguates a legitimately-absent value from a failure. The "
"exactly-one-non-nil invariant is the caller's convention — the engine types "
"the components and decodes them; it does not police the combination. A "
"return-position destructure (`let (v, ok) = f();`) is optimized so the pair "
"mints no cell where it is used as a pair."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:3
msgid ""
"rut's heap is **reference-counted**. When an object's strong count reaches "
"zero it is destroyed **immediately**, at a deterministic point in the "
"program. There is no collector: strong cycles leak by design — [weak "
"references](weak-refs.md) are the answer. Every VM owns one heap on one "
"thread and nothing is shared between isolates ([workers and "
"channels](workers-and-channels.md)), so the counters are plain cells with no "
"atomics and no locks."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:11
msgid "The cell model"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:13
msgid "Everything except primitives and `fn` values is a heap cell:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:15
msgid "Inline (moved by plain copy)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:15
msgid "Heap cells (refcounted handles)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:17
msgid "`u8..u64`, `i8..i64`, `u`/`isize`, `f32`, `f64`, `bool`, `nil`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:17
msgid ""
"`str`, `bytes`, `Vec<T>`, `[T]`, enums, structs, classes, trait objects, "
"`opaque` boxes, host boxes, `?T` boxes, coroutine frames"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:19
msgid ""
"Assignment, argument passing, and returning copy the **handle** (retain), "
"never the bytes. Mutation through one alias is visible through every alias — "
"reference semantics is the one default regime; there is no eager copy "
"anywhere. `bytes.clone()` is the **only** copy escape hatch."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:24
msgid ""
"There is no borrow syntax and no lifetimes: a handle simply keeps its "
"referent alive, so nothing dangles. Uniqueness matters only when "
"transferring buffers across isolates, where it is detected at runtime (`rc "
"== 1`) — never proven statically."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:29
msgid "Refcounting rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:31
msgid ""
"The compiler knows the static type of every register, so ref-aware ops are "
"emitted only where a reference can flow:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:34
msgid "Primitives and `fn` values move with a plain move — no counting."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:35
msgid ""
"References move with a ref move — retain the new, release the old; both "
"steps are exact."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:37
msgid ""
"Function boundaries pass references in registers; call/return sequences emit "
"the paired inc/dec. A loop over a `Vec<f64>` does no per-element counting."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:40
msgid ""
"**Overflow**: an increment past `u32::MAX` _immortalizes_ the object — the "
"count is pinned to the `0` sentinel (the same value interned literals use) — "
"a deliberate, logged leak instead of unsoundness."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:43
msgid ""
"Debug builds assert the full counter discipline: retain/release pairing, no "
"underflow, no double destruction."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:46
msgid "Destruction — `Disposal`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:48
msgid ""
"A type opts into destructor code by implementing the core trait `Disposal` "
"(see [Rc, dispose, and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md) for the trait "
"surface):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:52
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Disposal, DisposalContext };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Audit { log: Logger; n: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Disposal for Audit {\n"
"    fn dispose(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) {\n"
"        self.log.info(f\"released {self.n} octets\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn work() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"work\");\n"
"    let buf = Audit { log: log, n: 4 };\n"
"    log.info(\"working — 4 octets in flight\");\n"
"}   // buf's count reaches zero here\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    work();   // the boundary drains: dispose runs after main's body\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:80 src/reference/tasks.md:27
#: src/reference/reflection.md:97
msgid "Laws:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:82
msgid ""
"The engine calls `dispose(mut self, cx: DisposalContext)` exactly once per "
"cell death — when the strong count reaches zero. Types without a `Disposal` "
"impl release straight to the field walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:85
msgid ""
"The call runs **at a call boundary**, not re-entrantly inside the release: "
"death pins the cell and queues it, and the interpreter drains the queue at "
"its call boundaries — the root call's end for sync code, each drive for the "
"async weave — passing the pinned cell as `self` with a freshly minted `cx`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:90
msgid ""
"A dispose body may itself release references (a field walk that kills a "
"child whose own `dispose` is next): the drain loops until the queue stays "
"empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:93
msgid ""
"The context `cx` is engine-minted and empty today; it grows additively, "
"never by touching the trait signature."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:95
msgid ""
"Cancellation drops locals at the suspension point through the same machinery "
"([tasks](tasks.md)) — no special case."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:98
msgid ""
"Note the difference from finalizer-based runtimes: `dispose` always runs, "
"exactly once, at a knowable point. There is no \"later or never\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:101
msgid "Destruction order"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:103
msgid "When a cell's strong count reaches zero:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:105
msgid ""
"Every `Weak` box watching the cell is nulled **before any user code runs** — "
"a dispose body that calls `upgrade()` sees `nil`, deterministically ([weak "
"references](weak-refs.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:108
msgid ""
"The type's queued `dispose` body runs for cells whose type implements "
"`Disposal` (pinned cell, then released)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:110
msgid ""
"Ref-typed children are released **recursively**: record fields in "
"declaration order, sum payloads, array elements, `opaque` box inners, "
"closure captures. The walk is driven by a per-type release plan built once "
"from the type table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:114
msgid ""
"Host box payloads run their Rust `Drop` at the same point — sockets, files, "
"and textures die with the last handle, not \"sometime later\". An opt-in "
"`finalize` hook (no-op default) runs _before_ the payload's `Drop`; a rut "
"value the payload held releases through the same walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:118
msgid ""
"The block store frees the cell's variable-size payload; the slot returns to "
"the arena free list and is reused by the next mint ([the VM "
"heap](vm-heap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:122
msgid "Buffers, strings, and views"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:124
msgid ""
"**Primitive-element `[T]` buffers stay flat**: a `[T]` over numeric or "
"`bool` element types stores raw values inline behind the header. Element "
"copies in and out are plain moves — no counting. This is the one place the "
"everything-is-a-cell law does not reach."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:128
msgid ""
"**`Vec<T>` is not flat**: its backing is `buf: [?T]`, so element traffic "
"crosses nullable handles. `push`/`pop`/`set` emit the paired retain/release."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:131
msgid ""
"**Composite-element buffers store handles**: `[Point]` and `Vec<Point>` hold "
"one cell pointer per element; the buffer itself is the counted unit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:134
msgid ""
"`[T]` is a fixed-length cell; `N` is a compile-time constant and part of the "
"type's identity. Fixed-array literals that fold at compile time become "
"immortal constant-pool cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:137
msgid ""
"**`Slice<T>` view cells** hold a handle to their owner plus `off`/`len` — "
"never a pointer into the data block. Views dispatch through the owner, so "
"growth keeps existing views valid; indexing bounds-checks against `len ∩ "
"owner len` and **traps** out of range instead of reading garbage. Slices are "
"born only from implicit widening at an argument site; they are not nameable "
"or constructible in script."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:143
msgid ""
"**`str` is immutable**: interned literals live in the module's constant pool "
"(immortal); runtime-built strings are ordinary cells whose buffers are "
"internally copy-on-write — an implementation detail no program can observe, "
"because `str` has no mutation API."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:147
msgid ""
"**No interior pointers exist anywhere**, which is what keeps every heap walk "
"a simple typed walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:150
msgid "Internals"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:152
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"Header:  rc: u32        // strong count; u32::MAX = immortal sentinel\n"
"         ty: u32        // index into the type table\n"
"         bytes: u32     // accounted bytes, refunded on release\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:158
msgid ""
"The weak lists (referent slot word → its `WeakBox` cells) and the "
"disposal-armed markers (cells whose `dispose` is queued or has run) are "
"arena side tables, not header words."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:162
msgid ""
"Cell kinds: string, vec (growable), array (fixed), slice view, record (every "
"user struct/class — vtable + payload slots), enum (tag + payload slots; "
"dataless variants are immortal singletons), opaque box, weak box, and the "
"coroutine frames the async weave mints. Reified layouts are covered in "
"[reified types and layout](reified-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:168
msgid "The engine's two counting ops are exact and total:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:171
msgid "/* rc += 1 */"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:174
msgid "// underflow is a bug: assert\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:176
msgid "// order above; no suspect list\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:180
msgid ""
"Destruction is fully deterministic under the virtual clock, so a drop order "
"reproduces exactly in tests. Identity and equality semantics for cell values "
"are covered in [Rc, dispose, and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:3
msgid ""
"Reference counting frees an object when its strong count reaches zero ([the "
"Rc heap](rc-heap.md)). A strong cycle never reaches zero — so **strong "
"cycles leak until the `Vm` is dropped**. rut states this as a rule instead "
"of hiding it behind a collector: the heap stays trivially simple, "
"destructors stay exactly deterministic, and `Weak<T>` gives the two shapes "
"that cause accidental cycles — back-pointers and caches — a first-class "
"answer."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:11
msgid "Cycle policy"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:13
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:13
msgid "Content"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:15
msgid "No collector"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:15
msgid ""
"No mark bits, no colors, no pauses beyond destructor chains. What leaks "
"leaks wholly and predictably."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:16
msgid "Resource holders"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:16
msgid ""
"Classes holding resources (`Disposal` impls, host boxes) must **not** "
"participate in strong cycles."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:17
msgid "Back-pointers"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:17
msgid "Parent/child and observer shapes take a `Weak` back-pointer."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:18
msgid "Pure-data cycles"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:18
msgid "Harmless — memory only, freed wholesale at teardown."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:19
msgid "Lints"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:19
msgid ""
"The compiler may warn on obvious self-reference (a value stored into its own "
"field through a handle path); general cycle detection stays out of scope."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:21
msgid ""
"Cycles are possible through ordinary dataclass fields (`next: ?Node` "
"back-pointers) as well as through collections."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:24 src/reference/stdlib.md:74
msgid "`Weak<T>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:26
msgid ""
"`Weak` is a builtin generic class with one member. A weak box is a side cell "
"holding the referent's slot word, **unretained** — a weak never keeps "
"anything alive, and a path through a `Weak` does not close a strong cycle."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:31
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Node {\n"
"    value: u32;\n"
"    next: ?Node;          // strong — keeps the tail alive\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Node {\n"
"    pub fn new(value: u32) -> Self { return Self { value: value, next: nil "
"}; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn use_node(n: Node) {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"node {n.value}\");\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let n = Node.new(1);\n"
"    let w = Weak.new(n);        // Weak<Node>; T infers from n\n"
"    let b = w.upgrade();        // ?Node — a live handle\n"
"    if (b != nil) {\n"
"        use_node(b);\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:62
msgid "Construction — `Weak.new(v)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:64
msgid ""
"Construction is a **class method** with admission at the instantiation: `T` "
"must be a **reference type**. `Weak<i32>` and `Weak.new(some_fn)` diagnose; "
"primitives and `fn` values refuse. (The retired type-call spelling — a bare "
"call of the type name — does not compile; the diagnostic names "
"`Weak.new(v)`.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:69
msgid ""
"Works over **any cell**: a class, dataclass, `Vec`, `[T]`, enum, `str`, "
"`bytes`, a user `opaque` box, or a host box."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:71
msgid "`Weak.new(nil)` traps (\"weak on nil\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:72
msgid ""
"**`Weak.new(v)` consumes the argument's temporary** and nulls its register "
"(the engine's one consuming op): the weak observes the _binding's_ lifetime, "
"never a temporary's. `Weak.new(make())` watches a referent that dies as soon "
"as the temporary is released — `upgrade()` answers `nil`. Bind the value "
"first:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:78
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Payload { n: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"fn make() -> Payload { return Payload { n: 1 }; }\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let v = make();\n"
"    let w = Weak.new(v);  // watches the binding v — lives as long as v "
"does\n"
"    log.info(f\"{w.upgrade() != nil}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:97
msgid ""
"Two `Weak.new(v)` of one `v` are distinct boxes; `==` on weak boxes is "
"identity, and two weaks of the same referent are not equal to each other."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:101
msgid "Upgrade — `w.upgrade() -> ?T`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:103
msgid ""
"A dead-check plus retain: a live referent is retained into a fresh `?T` "
"handle; a dead one answers the **null slot** — true `nil`, never a box "
"containing nil."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:106
msgid ""
"On `Weak<?U>` (legal) the answer is `??U` — the sticky-`?` law "
"([by-reference and nullable](by-reference-and-nullable.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:108
msgid ""
"`upgrade()` on nil traps (\"upgrade on nil\"); on a non-weak value it traps "
"(\"upgrade on non-weak\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:111
msgid "Deterministic ordering"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:113
msgid ""
"The referent's death **nulls every weak box before anything that runs user "
"code** — before the dispose pin, before payload teardown. A dispose body "
"that calls `upgrade()` sees `nil`, with no window:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:117
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Disposal, DisposalContext };\n"
"\n"
"class Node  { child: ?Node; }\n"
"class Child { back: ?Weak<Node>; }   // the observer's weak back-pointer\n"
"\n"
"impl Disposal for Child {\n"
"    fn dispose(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) {\n"
"        // the parent's death released self through the field walk,\n"
"        // and the parent's weak list was nulled before any of that\n"
"        // user code ran — the back-pointer reads nil from in here:\n"
"        if (self.back.upgrade() == nil) { /* always taken here */ }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:133
msgid ""
"Weak references are **not** destructors — they observe; they never run code. "
"They exist for caches, observers, and back-pointers."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:136
msgid "Leak reporting"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:138
msgid ""
"**rc discipline** is asserted in debug builds: retain/release pairing, no "
"underflow, no double destruction."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:140
msgid ""
"**Shutdown leak report**: at `Vm::drop`, surviving non-immortal objects are "
"reported grouped by type, with allocation sites in debug builds — the "
"primary tool for finding leaked cycles. Deterministic execution means a leak "
"reproduces exactly."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:144
msgid ""
"The host can read live usage at any time (`vm.heap_usage()`, [resource "
"limits](resource-limits.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:146
msgid ""
"Fuzz targets assert no crash and that destructor counts match allocations "
"minus reported survivors."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:149
msgid ""
"Status: the `Weak<T>` surface above is shipped; the shutdown report and "
"per-type live counts are the specified diagnostics tier."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:152
msgid ""
"If real code ever demands more, a collector could return only as a separate, "
"opt-in module — the heap assumes nothing of it today."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-heap.md:3
msgid ""
"Host Rust code may use `std` freely. The VM's heap is the exception: **every "
"byte that backs a rut value comes from the VM's own heap manager** — "
"accounted, budgeted, pooled, and freed wholesale at `Vm` drop. No rut "
"allocation is an untracked `Box`/`Vec` somewhere in Rust land. The point of "
"the discipline:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-heap.md:9
msgid ""
"**Caps** — an embedder says \"this script gets N bytes\" and gets a "
"resumable `OutOfMemory` trap, not an OS abort ([resource "
"limits](resource-limits.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-heap.md:12
