msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:28
msgid "// host-callable names\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:32
msgid ""
"The `Surface` is the export record — functions, constants, types, traits, "
"impl registrations, and the builtin names `core` publishes. It is how a "
"_using_ module binds a _used_ module's members at compile time; since v16 it "
"is part of the wire format too — the surface section rides after `exports`, "
"and decode validates every row against the tables above it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:38
msgid "Wire layout"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:40
msgid ""
"The format is little-endian, everywhere — encoded words, hash inputs, "
"checksums. The first byte of a byte range is the least significant byte of "
"word 0; this is a law pinned by unit tests, not an accident of the host."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:45
msgid ""
"```\n"
"offset  field\n"
"0       magic \"RUTC\"                      # 4 bytes\n"
"4       version: u32                      # refuses any other value\n"
"8       name: str\n"
"        name table: u32 count, then count × str\n"
"        types: u32 count, then per type { name: IdentId, kind }\n"
"        traits: u32 count, then per trait { name, methods[] }\n"
"        trait slots: u32 count, then (trait: u32, method: u32) pairs\n"
"        vtables: u32 entries, then per entry { ty, [(slot, func)] }  # "
"sparse\n"
"        disposal rows: u32 entries, then (ty, func) pairs            # "
"sparse\n"
"        consts: u32 count, then tag + payload (below)\n"
"        funcs: u32 count, then per func (below)\n"
"        exports: u32 count, then (name: IdentId, func: u32) pairs\n"
"        instantiation ledger: type rows, then fn identities   # v17, below\n"
"        surface: namespace, funcs, consts, types + scope blocks + type\n"
"                 exports, traits, impls (both ABI lists), the reserved\n"
"                 inherent-impl table, native rows   # v16, below\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:65
msgid ""
"**Name table.** Names are interner ids everywhere — type names, field names, "
"enum members, trait/method names, function names, exports. The binary "
"carries only the interner's _non-well-known tail_; the fixed well-known "
"prefix (`self`, `nil`, the builtin members, the primitives, …) is implied by "
"the format. Decoding rebuilds the interner, so a decoded program is "
"self-contained; at link, each module's tail merges into the linked program's "
"table with the same rebase the type ids get."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:72
msgid ""
"**Type kinds** encode as a one-byte tag: nil, primitives, `str`, `bytes`, "
"arrays, enums (member + value pairs), records (field table), trait objects, "
"`opaque`, fn types, `?T`, trace, string builder, `Weak<T>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:75
msgid ""
"**Const tags**: `0` i64, `1` f64, `2` bool, `4` str, `5` type id. Retired "
"tags fail decode loudly rather than being reinterpreted."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:77
msgid ""
"**Funcs** serialize in full: name, param/ret types, `is_method`, capture "
"count, the typed register table, both operand pools, the op stream, the span "
"table, the parallel `(line, col)` position table, and the host-fn binding id "
"when the function is a bodyless thunk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:81
msgid ""
"**Instantiation ledger** (v17): the owner-anchored identity of every "
"instantiation the program compiled — type rows `(owner, decl, arguments, "
"type id)` first, then fn identities (the kind, the substitution values, and "
"the local fn id). Instantiation is owned by the declaring package, and link "
"unifies rows sharing a key into ONE program-wide row; a consumer resolves "
"its requests against a packaged binary's ledger. Type exports carry their "
"generic parameter lists beside this (see the surface below)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:89
msgid ""
"**Surface** (v16): the exported surface rides after `exports` — the "
"namespace head, funcs (with their async/host rows), consts, the carried type "
"descriptors + scope blocks + type exports (generic exports carry their "
"parameter names in order, v17), trait decls, impl registrations in both ABI "
"lists, a reserved length-prefixed inherent-impl table (zero rows until class "
"methods link), and the native rows with their ambient bits. Names are ids "
"into the name table above; decode rejects any id its tables cannot resolve."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:97
msgid ""
"**Versioning policy**: the version `u32` must equal the toolchain's exactly "
"— there is no migration or best-effort decode. A byte that changes "
"observable behavior bumps the version; artifacts from older compilers are "
"refused with the standard version error."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:104
msgid ""
"Same AST + same dependency versions + same compiler version ⇒ byte-identical "
"bytes. Serialization order is fixed (mount order, manifest order, pool "
"order), so binaries are cacheable by content hash, two builds diff to "
"nothing, and a trace captured against one binary can be restored against a "
"locally rebuilt one ([Diagnostics, traces, and "
"symbolication](diagnostics.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:111
msgid "Strip levels"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:113
msgid ""
"The symbolication data rides in every function: the `spans` table (pc → byte "
"offset) and the parallel `pos` table (pc → line/col). A build that skips "
"filling `pos` produces a binary whose traces still capture and render, "
"degrading to pc-only text. Names ride the interner; dropping name strings is "
"a publishing choice, and stripped traces restore through "
"recompilation-by-determinism (see [Diagnostics, traces, and "
"symbolication](diagnostics.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:121
msgid ""
"Both strippable halves are format-legal encodings, not special cases: tail "
"strings are opaque to the format (rewriting them to mangled `%N` names "
"touches no id, opcode, or table), and `0`/empty `pos` is the documented "
"stripped encoding — a stripped binary decodes and verifies identically, no "
"version bump required. The toolchain lane that produces and restores them — "
"the mangle/keep-set law, the private `.rutsym` sidecar, and `run --symbols` "
"— is [Symbol stripping and `.rutsym` sidecars](symbol-stripping.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:130
msgid "Type ids at rest and at link"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:132
msgid ""
"Type ids are program-global in a linked binary only because **link** already "
"rebased them. At rest (per module, pre-link) ids are module-local; the boot "
"table — the fixed primitive and builtin prefix — is shared by every module, "
"and each module's remaining types append after it. Every type id reachable "
"from a type descriptor, trait signature, constant, function signature, or op "
"operand is remapped at link, and `type_id<T>()` constants rebase with "
"everything else."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:140
msgid ""
"`type_id<T>()` values are `u32`, comparable, and unique per _instantiated_ "
"type within a VM run: `Vec<f32>` ≠ `Vec<f64>`, `[i32; 3]` ≠ `[i32; 4]` (the "
"constant length is part of the identity), and `Point` = `Point` across "
"modules — identity is assigned at link ([Reified types and "
"layout](reified-types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:146
msgid "Trait and impl tables"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:148
msgid ""
"Traits serialize with their method signatures; trait ids merge by name at "
"link — a trait imported in one module and declared in another is one trait, "
"and every `IsTrait` probe and vtable slot lands on the same global table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:152
msgid ""
"Trait-method slots are a global table of `(trait, method)` pairs; vtables "
"are per-type sparse maps slot → function id, merged at link. A trait impl "
"registered in any module reaches every call site."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:155
msgid ""
"**Impl registrations** `(trait, target)` merge at link; a duplicate pair is "
"a **link error** — per-module compiles cannot see each other, so the pair's "
"uniqueness is a link-level law ([Traits and dispatch](traits.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:160
msgid "Verification (load time)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:162
msgid ""
"Before any code executes, the verifier re-checks every function in the "
"binary:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:165 src/reference/bundles.md:279
msgid "check"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:167
msgid "register range"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:167
msgid ""
"every register operand \\< the function's register-file size; `NOREG` legal "
"only where an optional operand is defined"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:168
msgid "pool spans"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:168
msgid ""
"every `(off, argc)` span lands inside its function's `argv` / `labels` pool"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:169
msgid "type operands"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:169
msgid "every type id names a row of the type table"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:170
msgid "jump targets"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:170
msgid "every `jmp`/`br`/`brtable` target is in range"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:171
msgid "call arity"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:171
msgid "call argument counts match the callee's declared signature"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:172
msgid "enum members"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:172
msgid "`EnumNew` member indices in range, target actually an enum"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:173
msgid "host thunks"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:173
msgid "bodyless functions are skipped (nothing to verify)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:175
msgid ""
"A failed verification is a **load error** naming the module and function — "
"`function name (#i): op @pc: message`. Corrupted binaries never execute. "
"Verification is structural: it re-establishes what the compiler enforced at "
"emit time, so a hand-corrupted or stale artifact fails here instead of "
"misbehaving at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:181
msgid "The constant pool and load-time expressions"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:183
msgid ""
"Module-level `let` and `static` initializers are **folded at compile time**. "
"The surviving forms are: literals, enum members, operators over constants, "
"record literals (immortal constant-pool cells), fixed-array literals, "
"`Vec<T>(n)` / `Vec.from([...])` blobs, the layout builtins, and `Array<T, "
"N>.len()` with const-index bounds checks. A call to a user function in an "
"initializer is a compile error — there is no deferred evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:191
msgid ""
"`type_id<T>()` never executes: it folds to a constant from the type table. "
"`debug` position folding and trace symbolication are covered in "
"[Diagnostics, traces, and symbolication](diagnostics.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:195 src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:142
msgid "What is not here"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/module-binary.md:197
msgid ""
"A `.d.rut` declaration surface does not produce a binary — it has no bodies, "
"so it compiles to a signature surface only ([Host fns and declaration "
"files](host-fns.md)). And a `.rutbundle` is binaries plus their container: "
"the compiled packages ride as `.rutc` binaries (this format), splice-needed "
"packages and host pkgs as source groups ([Module bundles](bundles.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:3
msgid ""
"A publisher can ship a compiled artifact whose function, type, field, trait, "
"and method names — and source positions — are unreadable, while keeping a "
"private **symbol table** that, when supplied at load time, restores the real "
"names and line/col for [stack-trace symbolication](diagnostics.md). Users "
"holding only the binary see mangled names and pc-only traces; users holding "
"the sidecar see the real thing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:11
msgid ""
"The VM changes nothing: symbolication is lazy, per-index, against the loaded "
"program's interner and position tables — restoring is a matter of what the "
"loaded program carries ([Diagnostics, traces, and "
"symbolication](diagnostics.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:16
msgid "The model"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:19
msgid "# packed plugins/server -> server.rutbundle (...) + symbol table\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:24
msgid "Two files leave the build:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:26 src/reference/cli.md:157
msgid "file"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:28
msgid "`server.rutbundle`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:28
msgid ""
"the public artifact — every renameable name mangled, every function's "
"symbolication tables gone"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:29
msgid "`server.rutsym`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:29
msgid ""
"the private half — mangled → original name rows, and the span/position "
"tables per module"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:31
msgid ""
"The sidecar is **never an entry inside the bundle**. It rides beside it, and "
"whoever holds it may restore; whoever holds only the bundle cannot. It is "
"also not encrypted or obfuscated — see the limitations below."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:35
msgid "What is renamed"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:37
msgid ""
"Everything name-shaped a binary carries: function names, type names, field "
"names, trait and method names, enum members, and the exported surface rows. "
"Renaming is purely a **tail rewrite**: every name in a `.rutc` is an "
"interned-id index, and the binary serializes only the name table's "
"instance-local tail ([Module binary and verification](module-binary.md)) — "
"rewriting those tail strings touches nothing else. Ids, opcodes, vtables, fn "
"tables, and type layouts are untouched, so **no format-version bump rides "
"this**: a stripped binary decodes and verifies identically, and `0`/empty "
"position tables are the documented stripped encoding."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:48
msgid ""
"Mangled names are **`%0`, `%1`, …** — sequential over the sorted union of "
"renameable names across the whole packed closure (deterministic and "
"collision-free). `%` can never appear in an identifier or a package name, so "
"a mangled string cannot collide with any source-derived name by construction."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:54
msgid ""
"Mangling is one closure-wide string→string map applied to every compiled "
"group in the bundle — never per-group random names. Bundles link across "
"`.rutc` groups by name text at load (trait merge-by-name, the "
"instantiation-ledger keys), and a consistent map preserves all cross-group "
"identity for free."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:60
msgid "The keep-set"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:62
msgid "Some names are load-bearing and never rename:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:64
msgid "kept"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:64
msgid "why"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:66
msgid "well-known names (`main`, the builtin members, the primitives)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:66
msgid "never in the serialized tail"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:67
msgid ""
"host-fn registration names (`FuncCode.host_id`, `SurfaceFn.host`, host "
"thunks)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:67
msgid "the embedder's registry keys — `ink_host::log`, `rt:log`, …"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:68
msgid "export names"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:68
msgid "hosts call by string (`vm.call(\"main\", …)`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:69
msgid "package specs in instantiation rows"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:69
msgid "load-time unification keys"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:71
msgid ""
"`Program::name` (the module's display name in traces) is a plain string "
"field, not an interned name — it stays as-is. Local variable names never "
"exist at runtime (registers only), so there is nothing to strip there."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:75
msgid "Positions"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:77
msgid ""
"The pc → byte-offset span table and the parallel pc → (line, col) position "
"table leave **together**, per function, and restore together. Without them, "
"`StackTrace.line(i)`/`col(i)` read `0` and `render()` degrades to the "
"pc-only form — the behavior is unchanged, only the diagnostics lose fidelity "
"([Diagnostics](diagnostics.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:83
msgid "The mixed-closure refusal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:85
msgid ""
"`--strip` needs a **fully-compiled** closure. A package riding as a source "
"group (the `inline` flag, or a host declaration) would bind its compiled "
"dependencies' surfaces at load — and those names are mangled. The packer "
"refuses rather than guesses:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:97
msgid ""
"Host and declaration leaf groups have no compiled dependencies and pass "
"trivially. A refused world packs fine without `--strip`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:100 src/reference/bundles.md:359
msgid "CLI"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:103
msgid "# + out.rutsym beside it\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:107
msgid ""
"`--strip` writes the bundle as today plus the sibling sidecar "
"(`mod.rutbundle` → `mod.rutsym`) and prints both paths."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:109
msgid ""
"`--symbols` is legal only against a compiled `.rutbundle` — the source and "
"directory lanes compile fresh and need no map (a mismatched input is a usage "
"error). The table restores **before** the graph compiles, so linking and "
"every trace see real names. A section naming a module the bundle does not "
"carry draws a warning; a table from a different build simply matches nothing "
"and errors nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:116
msgid ""
"Embedders get the same lane as library calls: the packer's `pack_dir_opts` "
"returns the sidecar bytes beside the bundle, and `apply_symbols_to_session` "
"restores into a mounted session."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:120
msgid "Determinism and limitations"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:122
msgid ""
"Same input directory ⇒ byte-identical bundle ⇒ byte-identical sidecar (the "
"mangled numbering is sorted-union based, like everything else in the pack). "
"That determinism is also the honest limitation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:126
msgid ""
"**String values are data, not names** — string constants in the binary stay "
"exactly as the source wrote them. Stripping hides identifiers; it does not "
"scrub string literals."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:129
msgid ""
"**The sidecar is the private half, not a secret.** Anyone holding the "
"original source can rebuild the same closure deterministically and "
"regenerate an identical map. Treat `.rutsym` like a `.pdb`: distribute it "
"only to parties who may see names and positions."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:133
msgid ""
"**Stripping and the generic-source riding law refuse each other.** A "
"compiled pkg whose surface exports generics rides its source (so "
"consumer-spelled shapes compile at the link), and the ridden text would "
"recompile clean-named beside mangled binaries — the packer refuses `--strip` "
"on such a closure and says so. A fully concrete-class closure (no generic "
"exports anywhere) strips as before, and a consumer binding a stripped "
"compiled group's surface later is still a loud unification miss, never a "
"mislink."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-stripping.md:144
msgid ""
"`rut dump` on a `.rutc` with `--symbols`, and auto-keeping the shared "
"surfaces of a mixed closure (so it could strip anyway), are natural "
"follow-ups; neither ships today."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:3
msgid ""
"One struct, one thread, one heap. The VM (`rut-vm`) interprets [typed "
"bytecode](typed-bytecode.md) over an RC heap with inline destruction — no "
"JIT, no garbage-collection pass, no background threads."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:7
msgid "The machine"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:11
msgid "// the linked program\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:12
msgid "// the RC heap — [The VM heap](vm-heap.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:13
msgid "// saved frames (the call stack)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:14
msgid "// the ACTIVE frame\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:16
msgid "// per-op budget\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:17
msgid "// budget check cadence (default 1024)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:18
msgid "// embedder surface\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:19
msgid "// launched/parked frames to (re)drive\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:20
msgid "// sleep deadlines -> wake lists\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:21
msgid "// the VM's virtual clock\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:26
msgid ""
"A `Vm` is single-threaded by construction: workers are separate VMs "
"([Workers and channels](workers-and-channels.md)). Saved frames are plain "
"records `{ func, pc, regs, ret_dst }`; calls push, returns pop. Register "
"files are recycled through a pool, and per-function side tables "
"(reference-typed register indices, per-type field layouts, baked type "
"representations) keep the hot loop free of type-table lookups."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:33
msgid "The interpreter loop"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:35
msgid ""
"The loop is a `match` over decoded ops — `decode` is a table index, not a "
"byte scan. On native targets the default engine is a **threaded** dispatcher "
"(`rut-vm-threaded`): every op ends by tail-calling the next op's handler, so "
"dispatch is a jump and hot state stays in registers. A portable loop over "
"the same handler methods backs wasm and other targets; behavior is "
"identical, only the dispatch shape differs. Ops the threaded engine does not "
"absorb _bail_ to the match interpreter with the pc pinned at the op."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:44
msgid "Direct calls are an index + jump; trait calls are two loads (vtable row)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:45
msgid "an indirect jump."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:46
msgid ""
"Refcount retain/release are inline in the loop; a release to zero nulls the "
"weak list, queues the type's `dispose` body (the `Disposal` contract), and "
"frees ([The Rc heap and destructors](rc-heap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:50
msgid ""
"Fuel is accounted per op, with the fuller budget check amortized every "
"`interrupt_every` ops."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:55
msgid ""
"The embedder's host-fn binding table (`HostRegistry`) is built **before** "
"the `Vm` exists and is consumed at construction: every host thunk the "
"program declares is resolved against the registry immediately, so a "
"declared-but-unbound host fn is a _construction error_, never a mid-run "
"trap. The join produces one dense dispatch row per function — an adapter "
"code pointer, the body's state word, the declared return type, and its "
"is-reference verdict — so calling a host fn costs one table copy, no name "
"work, no allocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:64
msgid ""
"Host bodies are plain Rust functions over `&mut Vm` and slots; arity is "
"capped at 8 (the boundary's tuple law), and signatures are derived from the "
"body's Rust shape, not written by hand."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:67
msgid ""
"A host body records a trap in a VM-side channel rather than returning "
"`Result`; the trap fires before any destination register is written."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:69
msgid ""
"**Re-entrant calls** (a host fn calling back into rut) run the callee on a "
"fresh frame stack under the same budget. The outer cursor is stash/restore: "
"on return or trap the outer frame is exactly as it was, so a propagated "
"nested trap parks the outer frame _at the host op_ and `resume()` re-runs "
"the host fn. A host fn that prefers to handle the situation itself can catch "
"the trap, add fuel, and retry its nested call."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:76
msgid ""
"The async host-fn lane (`register_async!`) binds a Rust async body to the "
"minted `{scope}::{name}__start/__yield/__take/__cancel` rows the compiler "
"weaves for `host async fn` declarations ([The host futures "
"bridge](host-futures.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:81
msgid "Traps"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:83
msgid ""
"Bugs are traps, errors are values. Arithmetic overflow, division by zero, "
"indexing out of bounds, nil dereference, `panic(...)`, a failed downcast "
"guard, and budget exhaustion all unwind as `Err(Trap)` — never a Rust panic:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:98
msgid ""
"**Traps are catchable only at the host boundary.** `vm.call(...)` returns "
"`Result`; rut code never catches one — errors in rut are `(T, err)` tuples "
"([Errors and optionality](../tutorial/errors.md)). On the way out, frames "
"are dropped and destructors run: `dispose` bodies fire, borrow guards "
"release. The `Vm` itself survives a trap — the next call starts clean."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:109
msgid "// per-op countdown\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:111
msgid "// default 1024\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:115
msgid ""
"**Fuel** counts ops down. Exhaustion parks the frame at its pc — the loop "
"state _is_ the frame — so `add_fuel(n)` + `resume()` continues it; nothing "
"is lost or restarted. Loop back-edges carry `LoopHead` markers so tight "
"loops still park."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:119
msgid ""
"**Heap budget** is checked at every allocation; over-limit allocation traps "
"`OutOfMemory` the same parkable way."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:121
msgid ""
"**Heap accounting** is live: `heap_usage()` reports current bytes, "
"`fuel_used` accumulates across resumes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:123
msgid ""
"**Interrupts** are cooperative: the budget check cadence bounds how long any "
"host-requested stop can take. Native (host) bodies run outside the fuel "
"budget and are expected to be fast."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:126
msgid ""
"`Vm::new(prog, &limits, hooks, registry)` takes the limits; a default "
"construction uses unbounded fuel and heap — embedders that need budgets pass "
"them explicitly ([Resource limits and fuel](resource-limits.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:130
msgid "Coroutines and the driving loop"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:132
msgid ""
"Every async entry runs as a task: a root frame parked when `await` returns "
"pending. There is no microtask queue and no job executor — two queues and a "
"virtual clock:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:136
msgid ""
"`ready: VecDeque<Slot>` — launched or woken frames; each entry owns one "
"reference (retained on enqueue, released on pop)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:138
msgid ""
"`timers: BTreeMap<u64, Vec<Slot>>` — sleep deadlines against the VM's "
"**virtual clock** (`now_ms` / `set_now` — deterministic; the host advances "
"it explicitly, or maps it to wall time)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:141
msgid ""
"`drive(fut)` — one re-entrant call of a future's `yield` row: a fresh engine "
"context over the frame edge, answering `Done` or `Parked` off the frame's "
"state field. Fuel accounting rides the per-op budget unchanged: an exhausted "
"drive parks the frame at its pc, and a re-drive re-enters at the checkpoint "
"state."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:146
msgid ""
"`run_ready()` drains the ready queue one drive per entry; `next_deadline()` "
"expires due timers into `ready` and answers the earliest remaining deadline; "
"`pending_tasks()` is the idle test."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:149
msgid ""
"The wake pair (awaiter edge on the awaited frame, pending edge on the "
"awaiter) is one-directional and cleared on resume — no cycle forms."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:151
msgid ""
"A completed drive re-enqueues the frame's awaiter edge. A parked async frame "
"is simply not in `ready` until its wake edge or timer fires."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:154
msgid ""
"The engine never owns a wall clock: sleeps are virtual-clock deadlines, and "
"tests virtualize time by advancing the clock — full determinism ([Async and "
"await](async.md), [Tasks](tasks.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:158
msgid "Heap integration"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/vm-core.md:160
msgid ""
"Allocation, retain, and release are inline loop work. There is **no "
"collection pass at all**: destruction happens at release-to-zero, so nothing "
"ever interrupts a frame. Strong reference cycles leak by design; the "
"weak-reference machinery and the shutdown leak report are the diagnostic "
"surface ([Weak references and the cycle collector](weak-refs.md), [The Rc "
"heap and destructors](rc-heap.md)). Heap layout, cell formats, and the bump "
"allocator are [The VM heap](vm-heap.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:3
msgid ""
"Loading verifies, links, and mounts a program — it **executes nothing**. The "
"host owns time, I/O, and lifetime: the VM never reads the filesystem or a "
"clock on its own, and the host decides when (and whether) any rut code runs."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:8
msgid "From path to machine"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:11
msgid "# a directory with rut.toml\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:15
msgid ""
"The loader side (`rut-driver`) turns a path into a booted `Vm` in five steps:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:18
msgid "step"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:18
msgid "what happens"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:20
msgid "1. mount"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:20
msgid ""
"A **directory is one module**: its `rut.toml` names the package (`name`), "
"its entry (`entry.lib` / `entry.type` / `entry.libs`), and its "
"`[deps]`/`[peer-deps]`/`[dev-deps]` ([Project structure and "
"rut.toml](project-structure.md)). A `.rutbundle` mounts identically from a "
"zip ([Module bundles](bundles.md)). The graph walks `[deps]` recursively — "
"cycle guard, first-mount-wins, name-mismatch is an error — then runs one "
"peer gate over the closed set ([Dependency kinds](dependency-kinds.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:21
msgid "2. resolve surfaces"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:21
msgid ""
"Use paths resolve against mounted modules, exact and single-step: a package "
"name resolves or the diagnostic names the consumer manifest. A `.d.rut` "
"surface compiles through the checker and publishes signatures only."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:22
msgid "3. compile the graph"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:22
msgid ""
"Each module compiles (sources, in dependency post-order); `inline = true` "
"packages splice into their consumers instead of linking; host packages "
"synthesize bodyless thunks from their declared surfaces; a mounted bundle's "
"compiled packages push their decoded binaries at fresh, rebased scopes ([The "
"compiler pipeline](compiler.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:23
msgid "4. link + flatten"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:23
msgid ""
"Module-local type/function/const ids rebase into the global tables; the "
"shared boot prefix passes through; name tables merge; duplicate `(trait, "
"type)` impl pairs and duplicate module names are link errors. Cyclic use is "
"a compile-graph error, never a runtime event."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:24
msgid "5. verify + boot"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:24
msgid ""
"The load verifier re-checks every function ([Module binary and "
"verification](module-binary.md)); the `Vm` constructor joins the program's "
"host thunks against the embedder's `HostRegistry` — a declared-but-unbound "
"host fn is a boot error."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:26
msgid ""
"Loading a `.rutc` binary (`vm.load_binary`\\-style paths, and the wasm "
"runner) skips steps 2–3 and lands directly in verify + boot."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:29
msgid "What the host can call"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:31
msgid ""
"Callable names are the program's **`entry fn`s** plus the conventional "
"`main`. Their signatures were checked against the host-crossing rule at "
"compile time — primitives, `str`, `bytes`, `opaque`, `?T` over a crossing "
"type, and crossing tuples — so a bad surface can never surprise the embedder "
"at call time. An `entry fn -> (?T, err)` decodes positionally at `vm.call` "
"as a `(value, err)` pair (below)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:38
msgid ""
"Use paths never execute anything: importing a package mounts its surface; "
"only the host's calls run code."
msgstr ""

