#: src/reference/loading.md:66
msgid "The frame loop"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:68
msgid ""
"An embedder that owns an event loop (a GUI, a game frame, a web page) runs "
"one engine frame per tick:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:73
msgid "// input, network, ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:74
msgid "// drive ready tasks to completion\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:75
msgid "// earliest armed sleep\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:76
msgid "// host parks until then,\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:77
msgid "// then advances the clock\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:82
msgid ""
"`pending_tasks()` is the idle test — zero means the program has nothing "
"runnable. There is no job executor inside the VM: parking, waking, and "
"timing are these three verbs plus `set_now` ([Async and await](async.md)). A "
"program that mounts no async packages simply has no launcher; `await` stays "
"cold-poll inline ([The async model](../core-concepts/async-model.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:89
msgid "The soft-fail law (the err channel)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:91
msgid ""
"An `entry fn -> (?T, err)` never produces `Err`. The pair decodes at "
"`vm.call` as `Ok` — **with data in the pair**: a non-empty `err` string "
"means the turn failed softly; an empty `err` means success (nil value = "
"\"not found\"). `Err` remains exclusively the trapped turn: a bug, wiring "
"drift, exhausted fuel — the loud channel."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:97
msgid ""
"The law for host event loops (**the pump pattern**): per queued event, call "
"the turn entry, then —"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:100
msgid ""
"**`Ok`**: decode the pair. A non-empty err is _reported_ (surfaced to the "
"user/log, retained) and the loop **keeps draining**: the container survives, "
"the queue proceeds, the page stays alive. A returned err must never poison "
"the container or kill the drain — it is data the host acts on."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:105
msgid ""
"**`Err`**: the pump aborts **loud** — a trapped turn is a bug, not data. The "
"`Vm` itself survives for the next good turn."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:108
msgid ""
"On a raw JSON boundary (the wasm host), the envelope carries `\"err\"` "
"beside `\"trap\"`: a soft-fail run reads `{\"trap\": null, \"err\": \"…\"}`, "
"a success reads `\"err\": null`, and a panic sets `trap` with `\"err\": "
"null`. `trap` never fills `err`. Fuel and heap reporting are unchanged by "
"this."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:113
msgid "Parse-time robustness"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/loading.md:115
msgid ""
"The same embeddability pillar holds at the other end of the pipeline: the "
"[frontend](frontend.md) is stack-bounded with explicit nesting budgets — "
"malformed or hostile module source yields diagnostics, never a host stack "
"overflow, and a module with diagnostics never reaches the VM."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:3
msgid ""
"Errors are values and bugs are traps — either way, the first question is "
"_where_. The story has two halves: compile-time diagnostics (one model from "
"the first illegal byte to the last type error) and runtime traces (raw "
"captures, lazy symbolication)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:8
msgid "Compile-time diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:10
msgid ""
"One model serves every frontend and checking stage ([The "
"frontend](frontend.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:18
msgid ""
"Byte-offset spans; one renderer (`render_diags`) produces the caret form — a "
"primary line, secondary labels, notes — sorted by span so output is stable "
"across files and phases. The parser recovers at statement boundaries and "
"keeps going, so a file yields many diagnostics, not one. Compilation stops "
"before IR if any diagnostic exists; there is no warnings-only lane."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:25
msgid ""
"A compile trap worth knowing: name-level misses that involve [Dependency "
"kinds](dependency-kinds.md) peers have dedicated diagnostics (a missing "
"optional peer's integration is never a bare _unresolved name_)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:30
msgid "Runtime traces"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:32
msgid "Capture — raw pcs, nothing else"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:34
msgid ""
"`capture_stacktrace() -> StackTrace` (a builtin: the engine itself "
"implements it, compiler-lowered) walks the frame stack and stores raw frames:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:38
msgid ""
"the active frame innermost, then every saved frame outward, as `(func, "
"call-site pc)` pairs — about 8 bytes per frame;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:40
msgid ""
"**no name lookup, no source access, no symbolication at capture** — capture "
"is cheap enough for any error path;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:42
msgid ""
"saved-frame pcs are resume points, so symbolication backs one op to the call "
"site;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:44
msgid "target-independent by construction — the same walk runs on wasm."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:46
msgid ""
"The result lives in a dedicated immutable trace cell: a snapshot, shared by "
"handle, safe to store in error tuples. It takes no user impls and **never "
"crosses the host boundary** — a trace is a rut-side diagnostic object ([The "
"host boundary](../core-concepts/host-boundary.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:51
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"builtin class StackTrace {\n"
"    fn len(self) -> i32;          // frame count (innermost first)\n"
"    fn name(self, i: i32) -> str; // frame i's function name\n"
"    fn line(self, i: i32) -> i32; // call-site line (0 when stripped)\n"
"    fn col(self, i: i32) -> i32;  // call-site column (0 when stripped)\n"
"    fn render(self) -> str;       // the symbolication string\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:61
msgid "Out-of-range `i` traps `IndexOutOfBounds` — the index is a bug, not data."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:63
msgid "The tiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:65
msgid "tier"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:65
msgid "when it exists"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:67
msgid "`StackTrace` capture"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:67
msgid "explicit; a depth-proportional frame walk (~30 ns base + ~0.2 ns/frame)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:67
msgid ""
"opt-in at raise sites — a rich err carries `trace: ?StackTrace` only where "
"the producer decides the cost is worth it"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:68
msgid "`Trap`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:68
msgid "automatic at trap unwind"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:68
msgid "bugs, overflow, `panic` — the loud channel carries `kind` + message"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:69
msgid "`?` / err propagation"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:69
msgid "zero cost — no auto-capture on propagation"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:69
msgid "always"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:71
msgid ""
"Opt-in is the design: the cheap error path stays cheap, and capture is "
"pay-per-capture."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:74
msgid "Symbolication — lazy, per index"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:76
msgid "Members symbolicate **on access**, against the loaded program:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:78
msgid "`name(i)` — one interner lookup for frame _i_'s function name."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:79
msgid ""
"`line(i)` / `col(i)` — one direct read of the function's position table (pc "
"→ `(line, col)`, parallel to the pc → byte-offset span table; [Module binary "
"and verification](module-binary.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:82
msgid "`render()` — the only whole-trace pass, innermost first:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:89
msgid ""
"There is no eager symbolication and no frames array — a frame array would "
"mint rut-side data per access and fix the representation into the surface."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:93
msgid "Restoration paths"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:95
msgid ""
"A trace is captured against whatever is loaded — possibly a stripped, "
"published artifact. Four ways back to names:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:98
msgid ""
"**In-VM (the default).** The loaded program carries its interner and "
"position tables; every member call symbolicates from them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:100
msgid ""
"**Recompile by determinism.** Serialization is deterministic — same source + "
"same compiler version ⇒ byte-identical binary ⇒ identical pcs — so tooling "
"can symbolicate a captured trace against a locally rebuilt binary ([The "
"compiler pipeline](compiler.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:104
msgid ""
"**Stripped, no artifact.** Traces still _work_: they degrade to the pc-only "
"render form above, and stay restorable via path 2 later."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:106
msgid ""
"**The stripped artifact's private symbol table.** Name resolution and "
"position lookup follow the loaded program — so supplying a `.rutsym` sidecar "
"at load time (`run --symbols`, or `apply_symbols_to_session` as a library "
"call) restores real names and line/col before any trace is taken. The "
"sidecar's law — what mangles, what stays, the mixed-closure refusal — is "
"[Symbol stripping and `.rutsym` sidecars](symbol-stripping.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:114
msgid "Trap messages"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:116
msgid ""
"A trap renders as its kind plus message, e.g. `IndexOutOfBounds: index 9, "
"len 3`, `OutOfFuel: fuel exhausted — the frame is parked; add fuel and "
"resume()`. Traps do not carry captures — the trace surface is the opt-in "
"rut-side snapshot — but the host that receives `Err(Trap)` gets the kind, "
"the message, and (via `vm.fuel_used`, `heap_usage()`) the budget context "
"([VM core](vm-core.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:123
msgid "Where the surfaces live"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:125
msgid ""
"`panic(msg)` — the core builtin that raises; the `Disposal` trait is the "
"cleanup surface ([The Rc heap and destructors](rc-heap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:128
msgid ""
"`capture_stacktrace()` and the `StackTrace` class — ambient builtin names, "
"no `use` required ([core and the swappable packages](stdlib.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/diagnostics.md:131
msgid ""
"The web runner surfaces the same data in its envelope: `\"trap\"` beside "
"`\"err\"` ([Loading and the embed loop](loading.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:3
msgid ""
"`rut pack mod/` reads a module directory and emits **one file** — "
"`mod.rutbundle` — a zip archive carrying the module in one of two root "
"kinds, paired with the format version: a **lib** pkg packs **compiled** (v5 "
"— the root's `.rutc` binary, its scope ledger, and each dependency as a "
"compiled `.rutc` group or a source file set), and a `type = \"host\"` pkg "
"packs as a **decl** root (v6 — its `.d.rut` surface rides as source; "
"single-package). A bundle is the same contract as the directory it was "
"packed from, in one file: the loader mounts it exactly like the directory, "
"and the two compile to identical programs."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:14
msgid "Layout rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:16
msgid ""
"**Entry names are paths relative to the module root** — `rut.toml`, "
"`<pkg>.rutc`, `<pkg>.d.rut`, `<dep>/…` group entries for deps. No "
"directories otherwise, no metadata entries. Unknown extra entries are "
"ignored by loaders (forward compatibility) — except in a v6 decl bundle, "
"where a group entry, a root `.rutc`, or a ledger is a contradiction and "
"refuses (see [the v6 layout](#the-v6-layout))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:22
msgid ""
"The manifest's `name` is the package name the bundle answers to — a bare "
"`[a-zA-Z0-9_]+` name, the same law as directory manifests."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:24
msgid "Includes and splices never escape the archive: there is no outside."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:26
msgid "The v5 layout — a compiled root"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:28
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"rut.toml                 byte-for-byte; format = \"rutbundle\", "
"format_version = 5\n"
"rut.scopes               the pack-time scope ledger (scope = \"spec\" rows)\n"
"<pkg>.rutc               the root's compiled binary — bodies + surface\n"
"<pkg>.rut                the root's generic-bearing source, when its "
"surface\n"
"                           exports generics (the riding law, below)\n"
"<pkg>.d.rut              the root's surface text (humans, LSP), when it "
"declares one\n"
"<dep>/rut.toml           per dep group, mixed kinds:\n"
"<dep>/<dep>.rutc           linkable dep → a compiled group (its rut.toml "
"rides too);\n"
"                           generic exports ride compiled — the binary "
"carries\n"
"                           the closure's instantiations in its ledger\n"
"<dep>/<dep>.rut            the group's generic-bearing source, when its "
"surface\n"
"<dep>/group-*.rut …        exports generics (the entry + the [peer-deps] "
"group\n"
"                           files, verbatim — the riding law, below)\n"
"<dep>/<dep>.d.rut          its surface text, when it declares one\n"
"<leaf>/rut.toml            splice-needed dep (the `inline` flag) or a host\n"
"<leaf>/<leaf>.rut           pkg → a source group: the source file set\n"
"<leaf>/<leaf>.rut …         (entry, libs, peer groups)\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:48
msgid ""
"The **`.rutc` binaries are the linking truth**: bodies plus the surface on "
"the wire ([module binary and verification](module-binary.md)). A consumer "
"binds a compiled group's exports from the binary alone — nothing is rebuilt "
"from `.d.rut` text. The `.d.rut` files ride for humans and tooling; the "
"loader never reads them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:54
msgid ""
"The **scope ledger** names the pack-time scope of every linked module in the "
"closure (engine mounts included). A decoded program's ids spell its "
"pack-time scopes; the loader resolves each through the ledger to the module "
"to ensure and rebases the ids onto its own numbering — pack-time and "
"load-time numbering never have to agree. A reference with no ledger row is "
"refused."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:60
msgid ""
"**Mixed groups, one classifier**: the same splice law that rules the graph "
"(the `inline` flag — [modules and visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)) "
"decides each package's kind. Refuse, never guess: the packer calls the "
"graph's own classifier, there is no second implementation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:65
msgid ""
"**Generics ride compiled — and their source rides beside them** (the "
"generic-source riding law): instantiation is owned by the declaring package, "
"and the pack walk seeds each generic dep with the instantiations its "
"consumers spell — the binary's instantiation ledger names every `(owner, "
"decl, arguments)` row. A compiled pkg whose surface exports an OPEN generic "
"surface (generic fns, generic type exports, generic methods, generic-target "
"impls) ALSO rides the source that serves consumer-spelled shapes: the entry "
"lib, each `entry.libs` file, and each `[peer-deps]` group file, verbatim, "
"beside the binary. The entry lib's presence is the loader's dispatch marker; "
"non-generic pkgs (`http`, `ink`, `strbuild`) stay source-free. At the "
"consumer's link, a request the ledger lacks lowers the ridden text in the "
"consumer's session and compiles the monomorphized body with owner = the "
"pkg's spec — one row program-wide, indistinguishable from a pack-time one, "
"nothing persisted. A bundle with no riding source refuses such a request "
"loudly (`this bundle predates generic-source riding — re-pack it`), never "
"mislinks. `--strip` refuses the combination: the ridden text would recompile "
"clean-named beside mangled binaries."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:84
msgid ""
"**The root must be linkable** — else `pack: <pkg> is inline — its source is "
"its interface and it cannot be published compiled; share the directory "
"instead`. A root that has nothing to compile is not this refusal: a `type = "
"\"host\"` pkg packs as a v6 decl root, below."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:89
msgid "The v6 layout — a host root"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:91
msgid ""
"A `type = \"host\"` pkg's root IS its declaration surface, so a host bundle "
"is the manifest plus that surface, riding as source — single-package, no "
"ledger, no groups, nothing to decode:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:95
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"rut.toml                 byte-for-byte; format = \"rutbundle\", "
"format_version = 6\n"
"<pkg>.d.rut              the declaration surface — the root itself\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:100
msgid "The pairing is **total**, both directions refused at the version gate:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:102
msgid ""
"**v5 ⇔ compiled.** A host manifest at v5 refuses — a v5 bundle's root must "
"be a `.rutc` a host pkg cannot have."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:104
msgid ""
"**v6 ⇔ decl.** A lib manifest at v6 refuses — lib roots stay v5, compiled."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:106
msgid ""
"A v6 bundle carrying a root `.rutc` (`a host bundle's root is its surface, "
"not a compiled unit`), a `rut.scopes` ledger, or any group entry (`a host "
"bundle is single-package`) refuses: contradictions, never guesses."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:110
msgid ""
"Writers emit 6 **only** for decl roots — every lib bundle stays "
"byte-identical v5, and old readers refuse a v6 bundle loudly at the version "
"gate."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:114
msgid ""
"`rut pack <host-dir>` works (the surface verifies by parsing and lowering "
"once — the exact lane a mount runs — then rides as source), `--strip` "
"refuses on a host root (`no symbols to strip` — no programs, no sidecar), "
"and `rut run <host.rutbundle>` refuses with intent: a host bundle carries a "
"declaration surface — nothing to run; bind its rows from the embedder ([host "
"fns](host-fns.md)). The mount is the same lane a host group rides inside a "
"v5 bundle: the surface lowers into the pkg's host rows."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:123
msgid "The manifest — `rut.toml`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:125
msgid ""
"First entry in the zip; TOML; the same subset the directory form uses, "
"byte-for-byte the directory's manifest — which is what makes a bundle-shaped "
"directory pack unchanged:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:129
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"format = \"rutbundle\"\n"
"format_version = 5          # the bundle LAYOUT version — independent of\n"
"                            #   the module-binary version; a host root\n"
"                            #   declares 6 instead (see the v6 layout)\n"
"name = \"plugin\"             # the package this bundle answers to\n"
"entry.lib = \"./plugin.rut\"  # directory-time; the compiled form rides\n"
"                            #   plugin.rutc instead\n"
"\n"
"[deps]                      # the whole dep closure rides the archive\n"
"server = { path = \"../server\" }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:142
msgid ""
"Directory loading **ignores** `format`/`format_version` — a directory is not "
"a bundle — which is why the keys are safe to write into every module "
"manifest today: `examples/03-plugin/plugin` is a working directory that also "
"packs unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:147
msgid ""
"The rest of the manifest grammar — deps tables, `inline`, `[style]` — is "
"defined in [Project structure and rut.toml](project-structure.md); the "
"dependency semantics are [Dependency kinds](dependency-kinds.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:152
msgid "Url deps — bundles by reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:154
msgid "A consumer may pin a bundle by url instead of vendoring it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:156
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"[deps]\n"
"server = { url = \"https://example.com/server.rutbundle\", sha256 = "
"\"<64-hex>\" }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:161
msgid ""
"The mount is the bundle mount, one gate earlier: the call site's fetcher "
"produces the bytes (`DepFetch` — the CLI's is cache-first), then the loader "
"runs the same container/layout/name checks and — before all of them — the "
"**pin law**: the bytes must hash to the manifest row's `sha256`, on every "
"load, or the load refuses naming the dep, the url, and both hashes. The url "
"dep mounts as a **leaf**: its bundle brings the whole closure (every "
"declared dep satisfied by an in-archive group — the closure law is "
"unchanged, and a url row inside a packed bundle is inert metadata; loading a "
"bundle never fetches)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:171
msgid ""
"Because the manifest rides byte-for-byte, url rows carry into a consumer's "
"own `rut pack` output, satisfied by the rode-along groups: the archive's "
"groups re-encode from the consumer session's units (one `.rutc` path), "
"source groups copy their file set under the new prefix, and the same "
"manifest + pins still pack byte-identically. v1 refuses, loudly and named, "
"the cases that would emit a bundle the loader must reject: an archive source "
"group still declaring `[dev-deps]` directories (vendor the dep), a compiled "
"group the consumer's closure never uses (it cannot ride unpackaged), and a "
"declared dep that did not ride."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:182
msgid "The layout ledger"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/bundles.md:184
msgid ""
"`format_version` versions the zip layout and manifest keys themselves. "
"Loaders refuse a version they do not know **before reading anything else** — "
"refuse, never guess; the same gate makes an older loader refuse a newer "
"bundle."
msgstr ""

